  "response": "그때 뭔가 특별한 계기가 있었던 건 아닌데 어느 순간부터 그렇게 됐다는 거, 오히려 흔한 일이에요. 꼭 뚜렷한 사건이 있어야만 시작되는 건 아니니까요.\n\n다만 떠오르는 내용 자체를 보면, 대화에서 상대의 말이나 표정을 다시 떠올리면서 \"내가 뭘 잘못 말한 건 아닌가\" 하고 스스로를 점검하는 패턴이 보이네요. 그 패턴이 잠들기 전에 유난히 강해지는 거죠.\n\n그런 생각이 올라올 때, 평소 낮 동안에도 그런 식으로 대화 내용을 되짚는 편이신가요, 아니면 밤에 누웠을 때만 유독 그런가요?",
  "reasoning": "The client is describing a pattern of rumination - replaying conversations, others' words and facial expressions, wondering if they said something wrong or if others meant something different. This started about three months ago, and when asked what changed around that time, they say \"just... it was like that from then on\" - suggesting they can't pinpoint a specific trigger or event.\n\nKey observations:\n1. The rumination is specifically about social interactions - conversations, words, expressions\n2. There's a self-critical element: \"did I say something wrong?\"\n3. They can't identify a clear trigger, which is common - sometimes patterns just gradually intensify\n4. The \"just... it was like that\" response might indicate they don't want to dig deeper into that, or genuinely can't find a cause\n\nWhat would be helpful here:\n- Acknowledge that not being able to pinpoint a specific cause is normal and okay - don't push for a cause if they've already said they can't find one\n- The content of the rumination (social interactions, self-doubt about what they said) is significant - it suggests a pattern of self-monitoring and concern about how they're perceived\n- I could gently reflect on what the rumination is about (the social self-monitoring) rather than pushing for a cause\n- I should be careful not to diagnose or assume this is anxiety or social anxiety - just work with what they've shared\n\nI think the most helpful response would be to:\n1. Validate that not finding a clear trigger is okay\n2. Reflect on what the content of the rumination reveals - the pattern of replaying social interactions and self-questioning\n3. Maybe gently explore what this pattern does for them or how it affects their sense of self, without being too directive\n\nLet me keep it natural and not overly structured.\n",
